{"name":"French Bustle","hex":"#f2d5d4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"french","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To prepare food by cutting it into strips.","To kiss (another person) while inserting one’s tongue into the other person's mouth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/french"},{"term":"bustle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An excited activity; a stir. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A cover to protect and hide the back panel of a computer or other office machine."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move busily and energetically with fussiness (often followed by about).","To teem or abound (usually followed by with); to exhibit an energetic and active abundance (of a thing)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bustle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bustle","blocks":[{"text":"A bustle is a padded undergarment or wire frame used to add fullness, or support the drapery, at the back of women's dresses in the mid-to-late 19th century. Bustles are worn under the skirt in the back, just below the waist, to keep the skirt from dragging. Heavy fabric tended to pull the back of a skirt down and flatten it. As a result, a woman's petticoated skirt would lose its shape during everyday wear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Bustle_c._1885.jpg/330px-Bustle_c._1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bustle","wikidata":"Q535576"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/USpatent63234_1867.gif/500px-USpatent63234_1867.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/USpatent63234_1867.gif/1280px-USpatent63234_1867.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f